+## Retained original Job members
+
+The additive Windows member API keeps acquisition and explicit closure under the **same** original owner. It does not export raw handles, create a second Job, grant a product capability, or implement a C/Go member bridge.
+
+**Table 7. Member observation operations.**
+
+| Method | Outcome and retained state |
+|---|---|
+| `observe_job_members(deadline)` | Reserves one checked owner-local generation and returns it only after a complete nonempty observation. After reservation, failure retains an incomplete snapshot and every acquired handle, including a reservation with no handles |
+| `job_member_snapshot()` | Borrows the latest historical acquisition record. Census, identity rows and acquisition `complete` remain unchanged by revalidation/closure; resource close fields update on explicit closure |
+| `revalidate_job_members(generation, deadline, required_pid)` | Rechecks the whole original baseline through the same retained handles; an optional PID must belong to that set. Stale, closed or incomplete generations fail |
+| `close_job_members(generation)` | Attempts each acquired process-handle close once and caches the complete receipt. `Ok` with `complete=false` preserves real close errors; invalid/stale operations return an error. Borrowed original-root markers are never closed here |
+
+Acquisition queries the original Job's lifetime `TotalProcesses`, current `ActiveProcesses` and complete variable-length PID list [23–24]. Before and after observations must agree in all three quantities, with a sorted unique nonzero PID set and consistent counts. Lifetime total need not equal current active count. Native PID entries are `ULONG_PTR`, represented by `usize`; the parser uses the C member offset, native-width entries, checked extents and checked conversion to `u32`. Query growth is bounded to eight attempts, starting at 32 PID slots and allocating at most 4096; a larger or persistently changing Job is refused. That limit is an explicit experiment boundary, not an OS maximum. Equal reads do not constitute an atomic snapshot or promise future liveness.
+
+The original root uses its retained process handle and captured creation-time binding. It is not reopened by PID. Each descendant is opened once with query-limited-information and synchronize rights, inheritance disabled, and stored in the original owner before subsequent fallible queries. Every identity row requires the actual process ID, nonzero creation FILETIME, absolute image path, token user SID, membership in this exact Job and nonsignaled zero-time waits. FILETIME units and epoch follow Table 5; the copied SID remains observational data. A live descendant-only set after natural root exit is supported without overwriting the root result.
+
+One caller-supplied `Instant` is passed through acquisition or revalidation. It is checked at native query-helper boundaries, each census retry and before publishing success. A helper already executing, including its mandatory temporary-token close, finishes normally. No native syscall cancellation or hard elapsed-time bound is claimed. Entry expiry does not replace the previous snapshot or consume a generation. Revalidation compares current facts against the original baseline; it never reopens a PID or silently adopts a changed set.
+
+Explicit member closure remains available after a query failure or deadline. Receipt storage is prepared before consuming handles. Each owned handle is detached before its sole `CloseHandle` attempt; an immediate failure is recorded and the loop proceeds to other owned resources. Neither a repeated close nor dropping the owner retries a detached numeric handle. A finalized failed receipt drains the observation guard even though cleanup was not successful. Root-only and zero-acquired partial observations require this finalization too. A newer observation may replace only a finalized generation; callers retaining earlier evidence must copy it before that replacement.
+
+Admission/resume, termination, original `close_exited` and combined `finish` return `WouldBlock` while an observation is active, before consuming attempts or invoking the terminal action. Pure `wait_root` and `wait_for_exit` remain available. The existing `Drop` fallback still requests Job cleanup without claiming settlement.
+
+Qualification uses public lifecycle scenarios and narrow internal tests against real Windows resources. Internal stages are the same reservation, retention and identity-publication path used by public acquisition; restricted-access and close-protection substitutions occur only in tests. Parser byte cases establish structural rejection, not real process identity. Exact counts, negative controls and review findings are recorded in the [product audit](../../docs/modernization/verification/rust-owner-product-audit-2026-11-17.md#checks). The later Go bridge must finalize each native generation once even for root-only or zero-acquired failure; existing per-owned-handle callbacks alone cannot provide that guarantee.
+
+The final member qualification used the already installed Rust 1.100.0 on Windows/AMD64: **97 native checks in debug and 97 in release** (42 unit, 52 integration and three compile-fail examples), with none ignored or filtered. The existing C–Rust interface passed 22 tests per profile. No new Go member consumer was tested, because that bridge is not implemented in this increment. The final native source manifest is recorded separately from the later equivalent FFI test-iterator lint fix; the affected FFI tests were repeated. Earlier counts and Rust 1.99.1 results above remain historical qualifications.
+
+The fixture uses the Windows GUI subsystem: an initial CUI fixture with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` still created console-host members, and the production census correctly included them. No production PID filter was added. The final matrix includes the original stdio/crash tests. Four exact process-handle-count tests independently warm only the old create/finish route first. A separate control without the member API showed a first-use increase of three process-global handles followed by stable counts; the source of that increase is unknown. The assertions retain exact close deltas, repeat stability and final counts, with no tolerance. They do not establish that first use allocates no process-global resources.
